ead to +19.2. It never came back below +14.8, and the rest of the game was tidy endgame play.", "moments": [{"turn": 10, "text": "Opus 5.5 pushed in at H2 and lost 8.5 points, taking Black's lead from -2.8 to +5.6. The engine's first choice was F4, the point the 12k took next."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "The 12k played F4 and lost 8.6 points, taking the lead from +5.6 to -3.0. It was the only time in the game White was clearly ahead, and the engine preferred C5."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "H4 was the biggest early swing: 14.0 points lost, with the lead going from -3.0 to +11.1. The engine preferred G5, which the 12k took next. Even then Black's G5 lost 4.8 points and kept the game open."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "The 12k's G6 lost 6.1 points and cut the lead from +6.3 to +0.2. Opus 5.5's H5 the move before had lost 0.0 and was the engine's own choice, so the White column on the H file was holding up well."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The game was decided here. At +0.3 it was effectively even, and Opus 5.5's J3 lost 19.0 points, sending the lead to +19.2. The engine's first choice was H1. The rest of the game never changed the outcome."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "Opus 5.5's B6 lost another 7.5 points, though the lead of +22.3 afterwards shows it was not what decided the game. The left side then filled in with White stones on A6 to A8 and B5 to B7, and the lead sat near +20 until the final passes."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties with a danger flag, and count.py counts stones plus enclosed territory on a pasted board (optionally with named dead stones removed). Both only analyze the current position; neither loops over candidate moves, scores moves, or plays anything forward.", "stop": false}}